%% examples/flowchart-styling.srn — every flowchart author-styling directive
%% Siren accepts, in one document. Comment lines like these are stripped in
%% every diagram kind.
%%
%% A file of its own rather than more lines in examples/complex-showcase.srn:
%% that document is the flowchart animation showcase, and its 22 steps are
%% what a reader is there to follow. Styling 11 nodes and 14 edges on top of
%% them would leave two stories competing in one file, and every edge index a
%% linkStyle names would then depend on an edge order the timeline owns.
%% Here nothing moves, so what a directive paints is the only thing to see.
flowchart LR

%% A classDef only *defines* a named set of declarations. It styles nothing
%% until a statement applies it, so `stage` and `terminal` below have no
%% effect on their own — and either may be written after the statement that
%% applies it.
classDef stage fill:#3b82f633,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2
classDef terminal fill:#1e293b,stroke:#0f172a,stroke-width:2,color:#f8fafc

%% The `:::name` shorthand, written inside an edge line — on the source end
%% of the first edge and on the target end of the last.
Source[Source]:::terminal --> Parse[Parse]
Parse --> Resolve[Resolve]
Resolve --> Layout[Layout]
Layout --> Render[Render]
Render --> Output[Output]:::terminal

%% The apply-directive. Mermaid spells it `class` in a flowchart and
%% `cssClass` in a class diagram; both parse to one directive, so these two
%% lines and the `:::terminal` above are three spellings of one thing.
class Parse,Resolve stage
Layout:::stage

%% `style` applies declarations straight to one node. `color` rides beside
%% `fill` here: the box takes the amber fill and the *label* takes the dark
%% color, so an opaque fill no longer costs the text its legibility.
style Render fill:#f59e0b,stroke:#b45309,stroke-width:2,color:#1f2937

%% `linkStyle` is the one statement that reaches an edge, and it addresses
%% one by declaration index rather than by name. `default` is a fallback
%% tier, not a competing statement: it paints every edge no specific
%% statement named, and the two merge property by property — edge 0 below
%% ends up with the specific statement's stroke and the fallback's width,
%% because the author wrote that width once and never took it back.
linkStyle default stroke:#94a3b8,stroke-width:2
linkStyle 0 stroke:#f43f5e
linkStyle 3,4 stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:3
